  <figure class="deck-notes">
    <figcaption class="deck-ex-plate">Speaker notes · slide 7</figcaption>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Claim — the hinge of the deck: as designed today, Nearby isn't shippable. The happy path is well designed; about 40% of real-world use is underspecified.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The evaluator's verdict, in substance: strong concept and information design, with real gaps in error prevention, onboarding, accessibility and cold-start resilience.</li>
        <li>Cold start is the existential one. No listings → no engagement → no word of mouth → the neighborhood never activates. Strategy named it too: "cold start is existential."</li>
        <li>Map-first isn't a bug to patch. Map pins can't be used with a screen reader, so the primary interface shuts those users out.</li>
        <li>Also clean: no manipulative patterns found. One watch item — the daily digest could drift toward engagement optimization.</li>
        <li>Nine further issues are P1–P3 (duplicate listings, weak post-publish confirmation, the 15-minute confirmation gap, and others). None blocks launch on its own.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Source: /evaluate — UX Health Score, Anti-Pattern Verdict, Priority Issues E-01 to E-03; /fortify Stress Test Results.</p>
      <p>Transition: the good news is that none of these is open-ended.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </figure>
